        "description": "## What does this MR do and why?\n\nA repository's local config could override keys glab sets for itself, while glab wrote those keys only to the global file. A stale local value then won every read, and every write went somewhere nothing read, so `glab duo cli update` re-downloaded the same version on every run in that repository.\n\n`Scope` does not express this. `ScopeGlobal` means \"not per host\", and ordinary `ScopeGlobal` keys such as `git_protocol` and `branch_prefix` are meant to be set per repository.\n\nThis adds a `GlobalOnly` attribute to `KeyDef` and sets it on the ten keys the schema documents as automatically set. Reads skip the local file for those, and `config set` routes them to the global file. Either change alone breaks the loop; both together also stop new local values being written.\n\n## References\n\nCloses https://gitlab.com/gitlab-org/cli/-/issues/8571\n\nFound while reviewing https://gitlab.com/gitlab-org/cli/-/merge_requests/3981 \n\nRelated: https://gitlab.com/gitlab-org/cli/-/issues/8568\n\n## Differences\n\n**Before**, running the issue's reproduction:\n\n```\nglab config set duo_cli_binary_version 9.5.0      # local\nglab config set --global duo_cli_binary_version 9.26.0\nglab config get duo_cli_binary_version            # 9.5.0\n```\n\n**After:** the same sequence returns `9.26.0`, and no local file is created at all, because the first `set` is routed to the global config.\n\nKeys affected, all ten the schema already describes as \"(automatically set)\": `duo_cli_binary_path`, `duo_cli_binary_version`, `duo_cli_binary_checksum`, `duo_cli_last_update_check`, the four `orbit_cli_*` equivalents, `last_seen_version` and `last_whatsnew_version`.\n\nOrdinary keys are untouched: a local `git_protocol` still wins over a global one, and there is a test pinning that.\n\n## How to set up and validate locally\n\nEnd to end, the reproduction from the issue:\n\n```shell\ngo build -o /tmp/glab ./cmd/glab\ncd \"$(mktemp -d)\" && git init -q\nexport GLAB_CONFIG_DIR=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\n/tmp/glab config set duo_cli_binary_version 9.5.0\n/tmp/glab config set --global duo_cli_binary_version 9.26.0\n/tmp/glab config get duo_cli_binary_version\n```\n\nTo see the tests catch each half separately:\n\n* Change `if !IsGlobalOnlyKey(key)` in `GetWithSource` to `if true`.\\\n  `Test_GetWithSource_GlobalOnlyKeySkipsLocalConfig` fails with `9.5.0` where\\\n  `9.26.0` belongs.\n* Drop `|| config.IsGlobalOnlyKey(o.key)` from `writeGlobal` in `config_set.go`.\\\n  `TestConfigSet_GlobalOnlyKeyWritesGloballyWithoutTheFlag` takes the local path,\\\n  which `configStub.Local` returns nil for, so the write never reaches the config.\n\n```markdown\n## One decision worth a reviewer's eye\n\nThe issue offered two options for `config set` without `--global`: route the\nwrite to the global file, or reject it with an error naming `--global`.\n\nThis routes it. These keys are glab's own state rather than something a user\ntunes, so the value taking effect is what someone would want, and an error for a\nkey the user probably did not type deliberately seemed like friction. Happy to\nswitch it to a rejection if you would rather it be explicit.\n```\n\n## MR acceptance checklist\n\n- [x] Tests added for both halves, each verified to fail without its half of the fix\n- [x] Reproduced end to end against binaries built from this branch and from `main`\n- [x] Ordinary keys keep local-over-global precedence, with a test\n- [x] The key set is derived from `KeySchema` at init, aliases included, so it cannot drift\n- [x] Conforms to the code review guidelines",
